 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "How it rectifies",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "The chip judges the direction of current in the rectifier MOSFET from its drain-to-source voltage on VD. With the MOSFET off, the current runs in the body diode; with it on, RDSon acts as the sensing shunt. The gate is driven only while current flows from source to drain, and internal blanking keeps stray edges from toggling it at any load. It is meant for DCM and CrCM flyback and for resonant (LLC) half-bridge converters."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Turn-on",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "When the secondary starts to conduct, the body diode drives VDS well below VTH2 (-230 mV typ), and the chip raises the gate. VDS then jumps to a small negative value, usually with ringing. For the minimum on time set on MOT the drain comparator is ignored, so the ringing cannot switch the MOSFET off early."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Turn-off and blanking",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "As the current falls to zero VDS rises through VTH1 (-4 mV typ) and the gate goes low; any remaining current returns to the body diode and VDS steps negative again. To keep that step from re-arming turn-on, VTH2 is ignored for tBLANK (13 µs typ) after VTH1 trips. The blanking ends early only when VDS goes above VTH3 (1.18 V typ) and stays there longer than tBRST (400 ns typ); the chip is then armed for the next cycle."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Resonant half-bridge",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "In an LLC converter with a centre-tapped secondary each half has its own IR1161 and MOSFET. Switching follows the flyback sequence, with a sinusoidal current shape."
  },
  {
   "topic": "UVLO mode",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Below VCC ON the gate driver stays off and the chip draws only ICC START. It returns to this state from any other whenever VCC drops under VCC UVLO."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Start-up and synchronized enable",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "After VCC passes VCC ON the gate is held low for a delay of about 100 µs. Then VDS must rise above VTH3 twice before rectification is enabled, so the gate can never first turn on part-way through a conduction interval (which, with the minimum on time, would hold the MOSFET on past the end of conduction and let current reverse). Rectification then skips its first gate pulse, and per-cycle MOT checking begins; from then on the gate drives every cycle."
  },
  {
   "topic": "MOT protection mode",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "If a conduction interval ends before the minimum on time has run out (VD found above VTH1 at the end of MOT), the next gate pulse is withheld. This stops reverse current at very low duty cycle, at light or no load, and cuts standby power. The check runs every cycle, so normal drive resumes by itself once the conduction time grows past MOT again. It works the same in flyback and half-bridge circuits."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Setting the minimum on time",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "A resistor from MOT to GND sets the time, 2 × 10^-11 s per ohm: 24 kΩ gives about 500 ns and 50 kΩ about 1 µs. Too long a MOT at light load is what MOT protection guards against."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Logic-level MOSFETs",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "While the supply ramps up or down, the drain may already be switching with the chip still in UVLO, and below about 2 V on VCC the chip's gate sink is weak; meanwhile drain-gate capacitance can kick a logic-level MOSFET's gate past its threshold. An external pull-down is advised: a diode from GATE to the MOSFET gate path, a PNP whose emitter sits on the gate side of the diode and whose base is held on GATE with a resistor to GND, discharging the gate whenever GATE is low. It holds the gate under 1 V in all conditions."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Powering VCC",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "When the converter output lies inside the VCC range, VCC can be taken from it, perhaps through a low-value resistor for filtering. For higher outputs a clamp is advised, from a zener alone up to a linear regulator; with a clamp the gate swing is smaller too, which saves losses when VOUT is above 15 V. In the zener-and-NPN follower example the chip also starts late, only once the output exceeds VCC ON + VZD1 + VBE; the optional R1 and R2 set the clamp level more precisely."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shutdown",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "Pulling VCC under VCC UVLO turns the chip off. One way: an NPN on the base of the VCC clamp's pass transistor, driven through a resistor from a shutdown signal, pulls that base to ground."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gate drive layout",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Keep the loop from GATE through the MOSFET gate and back to GND as short and small as possible, to keep inductance low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Timing definitions",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "tDon runs from VDS crossing below VTH2 to the gate rising; tDoff from VDS crossing above VTH1 to the gate falling. Rise and fall times are taken between 10 % and 90 % of the gate swing."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Marking",
   "page": 24,
   "text": "Top: a date code (YW) and lot code (LC). Bottom: the IR logo and part number."
  }
 ],
